dsp_wavelets: Writing example code in C for choosing a wavelet basis involves defining the wavelet function and initializing the necessary variables. Below is a simple example using the Haar wavelet, which is the simplest wavelet and a good starting point for understanding wavelet transforms.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Haar wavelet functions
double haar_function(double x) {
    if (x >= 0 && x < 1) {
        return 1;
    } else if (x >= 1 && x < 2) {
        return -1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}

double haar_scaling_function(double x) {
    if (x >= 0 && x < 1) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}

int main() {
    int N = 8; // Length of the signal
    double signal[N] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8}; // Example signal

    // Print the original signal
    printf("Original signal: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        printf("%f ", signal[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");

    // Example of how to use the Haar wavelet functions
    // This is a simplified example and does not perform the full wavelet transform
    printf("Haar wavelet function values: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        printf("%f ", haar_function(i));
    }
    printf("\n");

    printf("Haar scaling function values: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        printf("%f ", haar_scaling_function(i));
    }
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines the Haar wavelet and scaling functions and prints their values for a given range. The `haar_function` represents the wavelet function, and the `haar_scaling_function` represents the scaling function. The example also initializes a simple signal and prints it.

To perform a full wavelet transform, you would need to implement the convolution of the signal with the wavelet and scaling functions at different scales and positions, which is more complex and typically requires a library or framework designed for wavelet transforms. The above code is a starting point to understand the basic functions involved in wavelet analysis.

